<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gulf of Mexico</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> is an arm of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a> bordered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, Mexico and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> and other great rivers drain into it, and explorers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-robert-cavelier-sieur-de-la-salle">La Salle</a>, who reached the Mississippi's mouth in 1682, used it to claim the interior of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. Its ports, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a>, linked the Spanish and French empires in America with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico.jpg" alt="Gulf of Mexico" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NOAA National Centers for Environmental Information. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gulf_of_Mexico_ETOPO_2022_(labeled).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> is an oceanic basin and marginal sea of the Atlantic, roughly oval and about 1,500 kilometres wide, with an area of about 1.6 million square kilometres. Almost half of it is shallow continental shelf. It connects to the Atlantic through the Straits of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> and to the Caribbean Sea through the Yucatán Channel, and the narrow connections keep its tidal range very small. Its shores lie along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, from Florida to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, along the Mexican states from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamaulipas">Tamaulipas</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintana-roo">Quintana Roo</a>, and along <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>. The basin began to form in the Late Triassic, about 215 million years ago, as a result of plate tectonics; before then the area was dry land in the middle of the supercontinent Pangaea.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest people on the coast found an arid landscape and hunted mostly rabbits, hare and rodents, with foraged plants. After climatic changes around 5,000 BCE, stable waterways, lagoons and shoals made fishing, agriculture and permanent settlement viable. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>, human remains date back to 10,000 BCE, and the Glades culture developed a complex society relying on fishing and shellfish. The Maya used the gulf as a major trade route and probably called it nahá, great water. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a> called it Chalchiuhtlicueyecatl, the house of Chalchiuhtlicue, the deity of the seas.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> but is not recorded entering the gulf. In 1517 Francisco Hernández de Córdoba discovered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yucatan-peninsula">Yucatán Peninsula</a>, the first European encounter with an advanced civilisation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. Two further expeditions followed, in 1518 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-grijalva">Juan de Grijalva</a> and in 1519 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a>, leading to the Conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. Hernández died in 1517 from the injuries and thirst suffered on the voyage.</p><p>European maps up to 1530 drew the gulf unlabelled. Cortés called it the Sea of the North in his dispatches, other Spanish explorers the Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> or Gulf of Cortés, and early maps used names such as the Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>. At one point New Spain encircled the gulf.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The gulf receives water from 33 major rivers on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> side. Its warm surface waters, averaging around 28 °C in summer, can feed powerful Atlantic hurricanes. It is one of the largest offshore petroleum production regions in the world and supports major fishing industries for the United States, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gulf of Mexico” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Exchange networks reaching from the Great Lakes to the <b>Gulf of Mexico</b> brought copper, mica, marine shells and shark teeth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cahokia">Cahokia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…34 Comanche leaders in San Antonio, Potsanaquahip (Buffalo Hump) led 500 to 700 Comanche cavalry down the Guadalupe valley to the <b>Gulf of Mexico</b>, sacking Victoria and Linnville in the Great Raid of 1840; pursued by 186 rangers, they lost their plunder at the Battle of Plum Creek.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-texas">Republic of Texas · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Olmec heartland lies in the hot, wet lowlands along the southern <b>Gulf of Mexico</b>, in southern Veracruz and western Tabasco.</blockquote><a href="/culture/olmecs">Olmecs · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>…into his office in Pittsburgh asking for a good two-syllable name for a Southern river; he turned down the Yazoo and the Pedee, and when the brothers looked at an atlas, Morrison's finger stopped at the Swanee, a small river in Florida flowing into the <b>Gulf of Mexico</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/old-folks-at-home">Old Folks at Home</a></li><li><blockquote>With Hurricane Katrina at Category 5 in the <b>Gulf of Mexico</b>, Mayor Ray Nagin ordered the city's first-ever mandatory evacuation.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-28">28 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In a coastal engagement on Lake Borgne, a lagoon of the <b>Gulf of Mexico</b> in southeastern Louisiana, the Royal Navy defeated the United States Navy.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-14">14 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
